.TH FSCANF 3crunch "" "Crunch" "Crunch Library Manual"
.SH NAME
fscanf \- read formatted input from a stream
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <stdio.h>
.B int fscanf(FILE *stream, const char *format);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
Reads formatted input from a Crunch stream. The compiler accepts additional
destination pointer arguments. Whitespace in the format consumes any amount
of input whitespace; other ordinary characters must match literally. %%
matches a percent sign.
.PP
Conversions support assignment suppression (*), decimal field widths,
and d, i, u, o, x, X, p, a, A, e, E, f, F, g, G, c, s, [, and n.
The [ conversion accepts ranges and ^ negation. Integer conversions and n
accept hh, h, l, ll, j, z, and t length modifiers. Floating conversions accept
l for double and L for long double; without a modifier they store float.
The c, s, and [ conversions operate on bytes; wide-character conversions are
not supported. Numeric syntax and whitespace use the C locale. The p
conversion accepts hexadecimal digits, optionally prefixed by 0x; the
implementation-specific string (nil) is not accepted. The nan spelling
is accepted without a parenthesized payload. Allocation extensions such as
%ms and positional arguments are not supported.
.PP
Field widths bound the input consumed by a conversion. For s and [, the
caller must provide space for the matched bytes and a terminating NUL.
For c, no terminator is written and the default width is one. Except for c,
[, and n, conversions skip leading whitespace. The n conversion stores the
number of input bytes consumed but does not increment the assignment count.
The first byte not consumed remains available to subsequent stream reads,
including on pipes.
.SH RETURN VALUE
Returns the number of assignments, possibly zero. Returns EOF when input
fails before any conversion completes. A null stream or format returns EOF
and sets errno to EINVAL.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR scanf (3crunch),
.BR fgetc (3crunch),
.BR fread (3crunch)
